      "side": 1,
      "message": "As mentioned in the API spec, I think these are unnecessary. Why treat federated service providers differently from any other \"services\", whether they are IaaS or services running on top of the cloud? They are all \"services\".",
      "revId": "06347c6bd855e90a5d43c48efd07fc7f30539432",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "da86d52c_18659fe0",
        "filename": "keystone/catalog/core.py",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 233,
      "author": {
        "id": 6482
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-02-18T20:40:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "the \u0027services\u0027 in the endpoint are specific to that openstack deployment, and follow a fairly rigid endpoint based structure. the \u0027service providers\u0027 we are listing here are not specific to the openstack deployment that the user has authenticated against. so I think adding them to the usual list of endpoints/services would muddle things, these are different, and as such should be treated differently.",
      "parentUuid": "da86d52c_1d3cb12e",
      "revId": "06347c6bd855e90a5d43c48efd07fc7f30539432",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "da86d52c_ef01de39",
        "filename": "keystone/catalog/core.py",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 233,
      "author": {
        "id": 1916
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-02-18T21:04:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think so. On the contrary, I think having a dichotomy of service providers and openstack services introduce unnecessary complexity. Fundamentally, an endpoint is just an url and a set of attributes (i.e. interface, name, etc) which uniquely characterize that url. Service catalog can, and should, contain endpoints which user are allowed to access. And that includes IaaS or services running on top of the cloud (i.e. DBaaS). Service provider is just another endpoint providing some service, which in the case it happens to be a Keystone endpoint provider federation APIs.\n\nClient does endpoint lookup using endpoint filtering mechanism, which is just a generic set of attribute-value pair. If we have the dichotomy of service providers in the service catalog, which is structurally different from an endpoint, that would also means we need to introduce additional and unnecessary service catalog logic on the client sides as well.",
      "parentUuid": "da86d52c_18659fe0",
      "revId": "06347c6bd855e90a5d43c48efd07fc7f30539432",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
